JavidTun
========

JavidTun is an Android client for the Xray core. It keeps the servers you add
in one list, shows how much of the subscription is left, and sends the traffic
of the phone through the server you pick. It installs under the name
app.nebulabox, and inside the code the app calls itself NebulaBox.

What is in it
-------------

  - servers from a share link, the clipboard, a QR image, the camera or a
    subscription address
  - one list per subscription, with a quota and expiry bar that comes from the
    subscription itself
  - routing with per app rules, and fragment, mux, dns, sniffing and the rest
    of the technical side under Advanced in the settings
  - an honest connection state: the button only turns green once traffic has
    really gone through, and a subscription that has run out says so instead of
    pretending that everything is fine
  - an update checker that reads the releases of this repository and offers the
    file that belongs to the architecture of the phone

Downloading
-----------

Take what you need from the newest release. Every file is named

    JavidTun-<version>-<build>-<architecture>.apk

with these architectures:

    arm64-v8a      most phones of the last years
    armeabi-v7a    older 32 bit phones
    x86_64         emulators and tablets
    x86            old emulators
    universal      all four cores in one file

If you are not sure which one your phone wants, take the universal file: it
installs anywhere and is only a little bigger than the rest.

The release also carries a detached signature for every file, the public key
that checks them and a list of hashes:

    gpg --import JavidTun-public-key.asc
    gpg --verify JavidTun-....apk.sig JavidTun-....apk
    sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS.txt

Building it yourself
--------------------

    git clone https://github.com/r4chan842/JavidTun
    cd JavidTun
    ./gradlew assembleFdroidRelease

You need a JDK 17 and an Android SDK with the build tools of API 35. The result
lands in app/build/outputs/apk/fdroid/release, one file per architecture with a
universal one next to them; without a keystore the files come out unsigned.

The Xray core is not built here. The build takes app/libs/libv2ray.aar from the
AndroidLibXrayLite project at a pinned tag and throws it away when its SHA-256
does not match; the tag and the hash sit in .github/workflows/build.yml.

The tunnel itself runs on hev-socks5-tunnel, which carries the packets of the
VPN interface to the local port of the core. The copy in app/src/main/jniLibs
is a build of that project for 64 bit phones; on the other architectures the
app uses the tun of the core instead.

Releases
--------

A release is started by hand from the Actions tab, with the publish choice set
to yes. Nothing is published on a push, so a release never happens by accident.

The version is written by hand in gradle.properties:

    APP_VERSION_NAME=2.1.0
    APP_VERSION_CODE=43

The second number is the one the phone looks at. Each architecture gets a
number of its own out of it, so the files of one release can never look like
the same app to the installer:

    armeabi-v7a    APP_VERSION_CODE * 10 + 1
    arm64-v8a      APP_VERSION_CODE * 10 + 2
    x86            APP_VERSION_CODE * 10 + 3
    x86_64         APP_VERSION_CODE * 10 + 4
    universal      APP_VERSION_CODE * 10 + 5

Bump APP_VERSION_CODE before every release, otherwise phones will not see it.

Bugs
----

Open an issue here. It helps to say which phone, which Android version and
which of the files you installed.

